 jbaker@halcyon.com  (James Baker) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-7-241:
>Does anyone know of a book that explains the opposite kind of prosthesis?
>ie taking neuro-signals out of the body to control things. For example
>artificial limbs. Or any research using outbound neuro-signals to
>communicate.

 I've been looking for info about this for several years, and these are
all I have found:

Eric A. Wan, Gregory T. A. Kovacs, Joseph M. Rosen, and Bernard Widrow,
 "Development of Neural Network Interfaces for Direct Control of Neural
Prostheses" in Proceedings of the International Joint Conference on
Neural Networks 1990, Vol 2.

They describe a device under development which could connect
microelectrodes to all the axons in an arm (at densities up to
2000 axons per mm^2). They give some ideas about how an artificial
arm could be trained to communicate with the biological part
of the arm of an amputee so as to behave like a natural arm.

Gregory T. A. Kovacs, Christopher W. Storment, and Joseph M. Rosen,
"Regeneration Microelectrode Array for Peripheral Nerve Recording
and Stimulation", IEEE Transactions on Biomedical Engineering, Vol 39, No 9,
September 1992.

