From: "Brady, Mark" <mbrady@msmail.his.tch.tmc.edu>
Date: Fri, 29 Jul 94 10:52:00 PDT
Subject: [#94-7-254] Houston's economy Was [RE: Venezuela & Socialism]

Since I am now living in Houston, I have to throw in my two cents worth.  I 
would say that Houston's economy is still dependent on oil.  Right now, some 
of the Majors are having layoffs.  However, it is more diversified than it 
used to be.  I work in the Medical Center and we have some 100,000 people 
directly or indirectly employed.  In addition, there is the Port of Houston, 
NASA, the banks and Compaq (and related industries).  The oil company 
layoffs are not expected to hurt the Houston economy the way they did in the 
early 80s when Reagan kept the price of oil down.

BTW Houston is the only major city NOT to have zoning.  The people felt, 
over the objections of the Mayor and City Council, that voluntary agreements 
were working effectively enough that government intervention was not needed. 
 However, just to be sure, the city charter was amended by a 2-1 margin so 
that it could not be imposed without a citywide referendum.  My local 
agreement says that I cannot drill for oil or keep livestock in my back yard 
 which is fine with me.

Also George Bush, Jr. is running against Ann Richards.

The big issue here is the Federal government wanting to outlaw assault 
rifles in the latest anti-crime bill.  Used in less than 1% of crimes, they 
are still high profile political targets.  I don't favor making them illegal 
for a variety of reasons, the right to privacy comes to mind first (I don't 
buy the NRA's Citizen's rights to bear arms), but I was wondering what the 
other opinions on the issue were.


